<section class="bl-hero" style="margin-top:68px">
  <div class="eyebrow">Writing</div>
  <h1>Building <em>in the open</em></h1>
  <p>I lead in public. I write about designing and building products with AI in the loop: the decisions, the trade-offs, and what actually ships. Here's the newsletter and the latest pieces.</p>
</section>

<section class="bl-sec">
  <div class="bl-feature">
    <div class="txt">
      <span class="tag">Newsletter</span>
      <h3>Building in the Open</h3>
      <p>A running account of designing and building real products with AI as a partner, not autocomplete. Practical, honest, and shipped. New issues on LinkedIn.</p>
      <a class="sub" href="https://www.linkedin.com/newsletters/building-in-the-open-7488094002198753281/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Subscribe on LinkedIn →</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bl-sec">
  <h2 class="bl-h2">Building in the Open</h2>
  <p class="bl-sub">The numbered series. One build, in public, including the parts that went wrong.</p>
  <div class="bl-grid">
    <a data-hue="build" class="art" href="https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/what-id-tell-designer-who-wants-build-aneil-razvi-t9g0c/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <img class="cover" src="assets/newsletter/05-so-you-want-to-build.jpg" alt="So You Want to Build, Issue no. 05" loading="lazy" width="1200" height="675">
      <div class="body">
        <div class="cat">Issue no. 05</div>
        <h4>So You Want to Build</h4>
        <p>The bottleneck was never the code. It was the judgment. Start before you feel ready, direct the tools instead of deferring to them, and tell the true story rather than the impressive one.</p>
        <span class="rd">Read on LinkedIn</span>
      </div>
    </a>
    <a data-hue="gate" class="art" href="https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/gate-aneil-razvi-3hrvc/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <img class="cover" src="assets/newsletter/04-the-gate.jpg" alt="The Gate, Issue no. 04" loading="lazy" width="1200" height="675">
      <div class="body">
        <div class="cat">Issue no. 04</div>
        <h4>The Gate</h4>
        <p>Handing your work to a reviewer you will never speak to. Two rejections taught me that some review flags are not bugs you patch, they are decisions about what the product actually is.</p>
        <span class="rd">Read on LinkedIn</span>
      </div>
    </a>
    <a data-hue="solo" class="art" href="https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/building-solo-ai-aneil-razvi-qh5ac/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <img class="cover" src="assets/newsletter/03-building-solo-with-ai.jpg" alt="Building Solo With AI, Issue no. 03" loading="lazy" width="1200" height="675">
      <div class="body">
        <div class="cat">Issue no. 03</div>
        <h4>Building Solo With AI</h4>
        <p>A passing build proves nothing. The thing writing the code cannot be the thing approving it, and everything the AI got confidently wrong looked exactly like progress.</p>
        <span class="rd">Read on LinkedIn</span>
      </div>
    </a>
    <a data-hue="wall" class="art" href="https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/wall-aneil-razvi-tmzjc/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <img class="cover" src="assets/newsletter/02-the-wall.jpg" alt="The Wall, Issue no. 02" loading="lazy" width="1200" height="675">
      <div class="body">
        <div class="cat">Issue no. 02</div>
        <h4>The Wall</h4>
        <p>None of these rules are hidden. They just do not announce themselves while you are designing, and by the time they matter you have already built the thing they forbid.</p>
        <span class="rd">Read on LinkedIn</span>
      </div>
    </a>
    <a data-hue="foundation" class="art" href="https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/foundation-aneil-razvi-hbfpc/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <img class="cover" src="assets/newsletter/01-the-foundation.jpg" alt="The Foundation, Issue no. 01" loading="lazy" width="1200" height="675">
      <div class="body">
        <div class="cat">Issue no. 01</div>
        <h4>The Foundation</h4>
        <p>Two days before launch a rule killed the product I had built. The pivot took an afternoon instead of a month, and only because I had written down why I made each decision.</p>
        <span class="rd">Read on LinkedIn</span>
      </div>
    </a>
  </div>

  <h2 class="bl-h2" style="margin-top:3.4rem">Sidenotes</h2>
  <p class="bl-sub">Shorter pieces outside the series, on whatever the build turned up that week.</p>
  <div class="bl-grid">
    <a data-hue="sidenote" class="art side" href="https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/your-design-system-becoming-rules-engine-aneil-razvi-f7ryc/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <img class="cover" src="assets/newsletter/sidenote-01-rules-engine.jpg" alt="Your Design System Is Becoming a Rules Engine, Sidenote no. 01" loading="lazy" width="1200" height="675">
      <div class="body">
        <div class="cat">Sidenote no. 01</div>
        <h4>Your Design System Is Becoming a Rules Engine</h4>
        <p>Localization and white-labeling force you to stop designing a screen and start designing the conditions under which one gets assembled. That is a rules engine, not a catalog of parts.</p>
        <span class="rd">Read on LinkedIn</span>
      </div>
    </a>
  </div>
</section>

<footer style="background:#0a1420;border-top:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.08);padding:40px 6%;text-align:center;color:rgba(255,255,255,.6)"><div style="font-family:'DM Serif Display',serif;font-size:1.2rem;color:#fff;margin-bottom:12px">Aneil <span style="color:var(--teal)">Razvi</span></div><div style="font-size:.9rem"><a style="color:#cfe;text-decoration:none;margin:0 10px" href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/aneilrazvi/" target="_blank">LinkedIn</a>·<a style="color:#cfe;text-decoration:none;margin:0 10px" href="https://cal.com/aneil-razvi" target="_blank">Book a call</a>·<a style="color:#cfe;text-decoration:none;margin:0 10px" href="mailto:hi@aneilrazvi.com">Email</a></div><div style="font-size:.78rem;color:rgba(255,255,255,.4);margin-top:14px">© 2026 Aneil Razvi · Allen, TX</div></footer>
